Sometimes, we do multiple logistic regressions in the first layer. For example, we can have two logistic regressions in the first layer: \[
\begin{aligned}
H_1 &= \text{logistic}(b_{11} + \boldsymbol{x}^\top\boldsymbol{\beta}_{11}), \\
H_2 &= \text{logistic}(b_{12} + \boldsymbol{x}^\top\boldsymbol{\beta}_{12})
\end{aligned}
\] And then, we can apply a linear transformation to the outputs of the first layer \(H_1\) and \(H_2\) and add an intercept term \(b_2\). The output of the neural network is: \[
O = b_2 + \beta_{21} H_1 + \beta_{22} H_2
\] The following figure shows the architecture


